---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
const stateName = "Illinois";
const stateNotes = "The original land trust state. Illinois land trusts are well-established with extensive case law. No specific enabling statute — based on common law and the Illinois Trust Code. Title companies and banks are familiar with the structure.";
const title = `Land Trust — ${stateName} | FreeTrustDocs`;
const description = "Land Trust in Illinois. Recognition status, statutory basis, trustee requirements, and how to create a land trust agreement. Free online generator.";
const schemaData = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://freetrustdocs.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Land Trust","item":"https://freetrustdocs.com/land-trust/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Illinois","item":"https://freetrustdocs.com/land-trust/illinois/"}]};
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>›</span>
        <a href="/land-trust/">Land Trust</a> <span>›</span> {stateName}
      </div>
      <h1>Land Trust — {stateName} <span class="utc-badge utc-badge--adopted">Recognized</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Illinois recognizes land trusts. As the original land trust state, Illinois has the most established legal framework. <a href="/create/land-trust/">Create a Land Trust →</a>
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/create/land-trust/" class="btn btn-primary">Create Land Trust Agreement →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Recognition Status</h2>
      <p>The original land trust state. Illinois land trusts are well-established with extensive case law. No specific enabling statute — based on common law and the Illinois Trust Code. Title companies and banks are familiar with the structure.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How Land Trusts Work in Illinois</h2>
      <p>
        A land trust in Illinois allows a trustee to hold legal title to real property
        while the beneficiary — the real owner — remains private. The beneficial interest is
        classified as <strong>personal property</strong>, which means it can be transferred by
        assignment without recording a deed.
      </p>
      <h3>Key features</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> The trustee's name appears on public records; the beneficiary's identity stays private</li>
        <li><strong>Probate avoidance:</strong> Beneficial interest transfers by assignment, not through probate</li>
        <li><strong>Beneficiary control:</strong> The trustee acts only on the beneficiary's written direction</li>
        <li><strong>Personal property treatment:</strong> The beneficial interest is personal property, not real property</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What You Need</h2>
      <p>
        A land trust requires two documents:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Land Trust Agreement</strong> — the private, unrecorded contract between trustee and beneficiary. <em>This is what FreeTrustDocs generates.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Deed to Trustee</strong> — the recorded deed transferring legal title. You file this separately at your county recorder's office.</li>
      </ol>
      <Callout variant="info" label="Important">
        <p>FreeTrustDocs generates the Land Trust Agreement only — not the deed. You'll need to prepare and record the deed separately, typically with a title company or attorney familiar with Illinois land trusts.</p>
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Trustee Requirements in Illinois</h2>
      <p>
        The trustee can be an individual or an entity (such as an LLC). For maximum privacy,
        the trustee should not be the beneficiary. Many people use a trusted third party or
        a professional trustee service. The trustee holds legal title but has no independent
        authority — they act only on the beneficiary's written direction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>How a Land Trust works in Illinois</h2>
      <p class="content-prewrap">A land trust in Illinois is a private arrangement where the property owner (called the beneficiary) transfers legal title to a trustee. The trustee holds and manages the property for the beneficiary's benefit. Two documents create the trust. The first is the Land Trust Agreement, a private contract between the beneficiary and the trustee. This document is never recorded in any public registry. The second is the Deed to Trustee, which transfers actual title to the trustee. This deed is recorded in the county where the property sits and must be notarized. The trust becomes effective once both documents are signed and the deed is recorded. Illinois recognizes land trusts under common law and the Illinois Trust Code (760 ILCS 3/101). There is no specific land-trust enabling statute. Title companies and banks in Illinois are familiar with this structure. For mortgaged property, the Garn-St. Germain Act generally protects transfers to inter vivos trusts where the borrower remains a beneficiary, though lenders may interpret this differently.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <p class="content-prewrap">One common mistake is recording the Land Trust Agreement. It is meant to stay private and never be filed in any public office. Recording it defeats the privacy purpose. Another error is using a deed that is not notarized before recording. The Deed to Trustee must be notarized to be valid in Illinois county recorders' offices. Some people also try to use a land trust as the sole estate-planning document, but a land trust only controls real property. It does not cover bank accounts, investments, or personal property. A further mistake is assuming the trust protects against all creditor claims. A recorded judgment may still lead to a fraudulent conveyance challenge. Finally, failing to check the mortgage due-on-sale clause before transferring property into a trust can trigger a loan acceleration.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Is a land trust the same as a living trust?</h3>
      <p class="content-prewrap">No. A living trust is a probate-avoidance tool that holds all types of assets, including bank accounts and investments. A land trust is a specific type of trust that holds only real property. In Illinois, land trusts are governed by common law and the Illinois Trust Code, not by the Illinois Probate Act. The structure focuses on privacy and ease of transfer of beneficial interest, which is different from a living trust's broader estate planning function.</p>
      <h3>Does recording the Deed to Trustee make my ownership public?</h3>
      <p class="content-prewrap">The Deed to Trustee is recorded in the county recorder's office, but it lists the trustee as the legal owner, not the beneficiary. The Land Trust Agreement, which names the beneficiary, remains private and is never recorded. Anyone looking at the public record sees the trustee's name, not the beneficiary's. This separation is the core privacy mechanism of the Illinois land trust.</p>
      <h3>Can a land trust protect property from creditors?</h3>
      <p class="content-prewrap">A recorded judgment against a beneficiary does not automatically create a lien on the property held in a land trust. Because the trustee holds legal title, a creditor must first establish that the trust is a fraudulent conveyance before reaching the property. This is not automatic protection. The Illinois Trust Code (760 ILCS 3/101) provides the framework, but case law in Illinois has addressed creditor claims on a case-by-case basis. The trust does not shield property from valid claims or judgments.</p>
      <h3>What happens to the property when the beneficiary dies?</h3>
      <p class="content-prewrap">The beneficial interest in a land trust can be transferred upon death without going through probate court. The beneficiary may assign the beneficial interest to a successor by a private written document. No new deed needs to be recorded for this transfer. The successor beneficiary then steps into the shoes of the original beneficiary. This avoids the time and cost of probate for real property in Illinois. The trust agreement itself may include provisions for succession, or the beneficiary may use a separate assignment document.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What makes Illinois different</h2>
      <p class="content-prewrap">Illinois is the original land trust state, and land trusts here are well-established with extensive case law backing. The Illinois Trust Code (760 ILCS 3/101) provides the statutory framework, though land trusts themselves are common-law instruments with no specific enabling statute. Title companies and banks in Illinois are familiar with land trusts and routinely handle transactions involving them. The two-document structure â a private trust agreement and a recorded deed to trustee â is standard across the state. No witness requirements exist beyond the notarization of the deed. For mortgaged property, the Garn-St. Germain Act generally protects transfers to inter vivos trusts where the borrower remains a beneficiary, but lenders may interpret this differently. Each county recorder's office may have slightly different formatting requirements for the Deed to Trustee.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-tight" id="sources">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Sources &amp; verification</h2>
      <p class="sources-line">Statutory references verified against official state legislature sources on 2026-09-17.</p>
